## Numbers Don't Lie
This year alone has seen 509 recalls, with 152 categorized as Class I--the most severe. Salmonella, a bacterial contaminant often linked with raw or unpasteurized products, has been responsible for 156 of these recalls. The year 2026 has already surpassed mid-point, and a series of food recalls points to recurring issues in food safety protocols.

## Milk Powder Mayhem
This week's batch of recalls is headlined by contamination in milk powder supplied by California Dairies. Salmonella fears prompted a cascade of recalls from various brands, including Legacy Bakehouse LLC. Their affected products range from Butter Parsley Bagel Crisps to Parmesan & Herb Bagel Crisps. It's clear that a single compromised ingredient can have widespread ramifications when used by multiple producers.

## Allergen Omissions
Wawa Beverage Company joins the recall list with several of its diet beverages, including iced tea and lemonade, which were found to contain undeclared milk allergens. These products could potentially cause severe reactions in individuals with milk allergies, a risk that is entirely preventable through proper labeling.

## Brands in Hot Water
Among the top offenders by recall count, Moonlight Packing Corporation, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, and Albertsons Companies LLC are leading the pack with significant histories of Class I recalls. In our updated tally, Moonlight Packing Corporation stands out with 11 recalls this year alone, all classified as Class I--a clear indication of a need for better safety measures.

## Looking Ahead
As consumers, staying informed is our first defense. The frequency of food recalls underscores the importance of vigilance and awareness when choosing products. We encourage you to check recall notices regularly and remain proactive about the food you consume.
